00:49:57,890 --> 00:49:59,170
it will trigger a query.

991
00:49:59,170 --> 00:50:02,470
It will generate a
search query of what

992
00:50:02,470 --> 00:50:07,030
tool calls to generate
and go search things over.

993
00:50:07,030 --> 00:50:09,230
And there will be
multiple iterations

994
00:50:09,230 --> 00:50:12,710
in a single reasoning session of
like fetching these documents.

995
00:50:12,710 --> 00:50:14,810
And the second thing
it will do differently,

996
00:50:14,810 --> 00:50:16,850
and I will show
examples of that,

997
00:50:16,850 --> 00:50:19,210
is that it will analyze
the retrieved documents.

998
00:50:19,210 --> 00:50:22,847
So instead of just dumping what
it retrieved as the document,

999
00:50:22,847 --> 00:50:24,430
it will analyze the
retrieved document

1000
00:50:24,430 --> 00:50:28,430
and extract relevant chunks
of information from there.

1001
00:50:28,430 --> 00:50:32,830
And it will only put
these relevant information

1002
00:50:32,830 --> 00:50:36,590
into the prompts so
that it integrates well

1003
00:50:36,590 --> 00:50:40,230
into the reasoning chain.

1004
00:50:40,230 --> 00:50:44,230
So just to reiterate
how this works--

1005
00:50:44,230 --> 00:50:45,870
so this is an original
question, which

1006
00:50:45,870 --> 00:50:48,910
is a chemistry question,
which is talking about--

1007
00:50:48,910 --> 00:50:52,470
it wants to get the carbon
atoms count of product 3

1008
00:50:52,470 --> 00:50:56,540
which is output of a bunch of
different chemical reactions.

1009
00:50:56,540 --> 00:50:59,740
So if you were to use a
large reasoning model,

1010
00:50:59,740 --> 00:51:01,700
you would ask the model
to start thinking.

1011
00:51:01,700 --> 00:51:04,500
It looks at this term,
which I don't understand,

1012
00:51:04,500 --> 00:51:06,280
and perhaps the model
doesn't understand.

1013
00:51:06,280 --> 00:51:10,340
So it will go ask a
lookup, what does it mean?

1014
00:51:10,340 --> 00:51:12,760
Now, one trick is that
it doesn't go look up,

1015
00:51:12,760 --> 00:51:15,340
so it makes a guess and
comes up with whatever

1016
00:51:15,340 --> 00:51:17,740
is in its knowledge base
in the stored weights

1017
00:51:17,740 --> 00:51:21,120
and provides a final answer
based on what it guessed.

1018
00:51:21,120 --> 00:51:24,760
But if there is a guess here,
and it got the answer wrong,

1019
00:51:24,760 --> 00:51:26,700
then those things
will cascade as errors

1020
00:51:26,700 --> 00:51:28,660
into whatever is
the final answer.

1021
00:51:28,660 --> 00:51:31,100
So all the perhaps or
all the uncertainty

1022
00:51:31,100 --> 00:51:33,120
will basically cascade
into the final answer.

1023
00:51:33,120 --> 00:51:37,100
So your certainty for
the final answer is low.

1024
00:51:37,100 --> 00:51:39,060
Now, one possibility
is that you basically--

1025
00:51:39,060 --> 00:51:41,320
whenever you encounter
unfamiliar knowledge,

1026
00:51:41,320 --> 00:51:42,760
you can now do web searches.

1027
00:51:42,760 --> 00:51:45,300
So that would be
a simple scenario

1028
00:51:45,300 --> 00:51:48,900
of like OK, unfamiliar
term, basically go and do

1029
00:51:48,900 --> 00:51:53,880
a tool call for search, return
the document that basically

1030
00:51:53,880 --> 00:51:56,620
has that search tool for perhaps
there is a Wikipedia page,

1031
00:51:56,620 --> 00:51:59,080
or there's a web page
that has that information,

1032
00:51:59,080 --> 00:52:03,040
and put that in the reasoning
chain, and now do the--

1033
00:52:03,040 --> 00:52:06,120
put that in the prompt, and
then do the call again, and then

1034
00:52:06,120 --> 00:52:08,880
provide the final
answer based on that.

1035
00:52:08,880 --> 00:52:12,160
The only challenge with this
is that as you basically

1036
00:52:12,160 --> 00:52:14,940
increase your amount of
content you're putting there,

1037
00:52:14,940 --> 00:52:17,440
there might be not as
much relevant content

1038
00:52:17,440 --> 00:52:19,020
in these documents.

1039
00:52:19,020 --> 00:52:21,600
Like if you went and fetched
like 10 documents that

1040
00:52:21,600 --> 00:52:24,400
were related to the
information, there's

1041
00:52:24,400 --> 00:52:26,500
too much information for
the model to process,

1042
00:52:26,500 --> 00:52:28,860
so it might actually not
still give the correct answer.

1043
00:52:28,860 --> 00:52:32,120
So in this particular case,
it was like 10 carbon atoms

1044
00:52:32,120 --> 00:52:33,100
in product 3.

1045
00:52:33,100 --> 00:52:34,460
Here's 14 carbon atoms.

1046
00:52:34,460 --> 00:52:38,480
So it still got it wrong.

1047
00:52:38,480 --> 00:52:41,920
What Search-o1 is proposing
is that now you have--

1048
00:52:41,920 --> 00:52:44,080
whenever you get the
unfamiliar knowledge,

1049
00:52:44,080 --> 00:52:47,420
it will go and search
for helpful information.

1050
00:52:47,420 --> 00:52:50,320
So that's still a tool
call to your search.

1051
00:52:50,320 --> 00:52:53,730
But then, it will reason
within the document.

1052
00:52:53,730 --> 00:52:56,070
So it will try to look
at these documents

1053
00:52:56,070 --> 00:52:58,490
and see if this is helpful
information or not,

1054
00:52:58,490 --> 00:53:01,910
extract the relevant
content, and then put it back

1055
00:53:01,910 --> 00:53:05,230
in the next prompt, like
what is the relevant chunk

1056
00:53:05,230 --> 00:53:06,870
of information to put?

1057
00:53:06,870 --> 00:53:10,110
And that will allow the model
to continue coherent reasoning

1058
00:53:10,110 --> 00:53:11,690
and provide the
final answer here.

1059
00:53:11,690 --> 00:53:13,670
So in this particular
case, basically,

1060
00:53:13,670 --> 00:53:17,470
the ability to do this
summarization of information

1061
00:53:17,470 --> 00:53:20,470
or reason within the
document really improves

1062
00:53:20,470 --> 00:53:23,310
the quality of life, how it
gets to the final answer instead

1063
00:53:23,310 --> 00:53:29,350
of clustering the prompt with
10 documents or 20 documents.

1064
00:53:29,350 --> 00:53:31,350
Questions on what's
the difference

1065
00:53:31,350 --> 00:53:32,730
between the three approaches?

1066
00:53:36,950 --> 00:53:37,830
Yes.

1067
00:53:37,830 --> 00:53:40,310
Why in the final leg there
does it say-- in the first one,

1068
00:53:40,310 --> 00:53:41,710
it says--

1069
00:53:41,710 --> 00:53:44,670
or perhaps, it may look at
as far as this little one.

1070
00:53:44,670 --> 00:53:46,270
Does it concern you?

1071
00:53:46,270 --> 00:53:48,130
Because it's been
given the information.

1072
00:53:48,130 --> 00:53:50,410
So the uncertainty has reduced.

1073
00:53:50,410 --> 00:53:53,610
It has actually been-- it's not
even saying that in the middle

1074
00:53:53,610 --> 00:53:54,490
one, which--

1075
00:53:54,490 --> 00:53:59,410
because it's been given the
right information in the prompt.

1076
00:53:59,410 --> 00:54:05,250
I think the harder part is, how
does it know what to search for?

1077
00:54:05,250 --> 00:54:07,630
It needs to know which
keywords it does not know.

1078
00:54:12,450 --> 00:54:15,574
That's the question I would ask.

1079
00:54:15,574 --> 00:54:17,890
It gets to know his answer.

1080
00:54:17,890 --> 00:54:19,490
The answer is I
think you basically

1081
00:54:19,490 --> 00:54:23,410
need to break down what are the
key-- so there is different ways

1082
00:54:23,410 --> 00:54:25,890
to tackle that question, right?

1083
00:54:25,890 --> 00:54:28,370
If you're building
a real application,

1084
00:54:28,370 --> 00:54:32,130
you want to identify
which parts--

1085
00:54:32,130 --> 00:54:34,628
you don't want to rely on the
knowledge base of the LLM.

1086
00:54:34,628 --> 00:54:36,170
And you actually do
want to do a tool

1087
00:54:36,170 --> 00:54:39,210
call because that
information is likely not

1088
00:54:39,210 --> 00:54:43,450
what you want to trust the
weights of the LLM for.

1089
00:54:43,450 --> 00:54:46,370
It's almost like you're
finding the key entities

1090
00:54:46,370 --> 00:54:51,340
in your question and fetching
information on those.

1091
00:54:59,520 --> 00:55:00,360
OK.

1092
00:55:00,360 --> 00:55:02,660
So there were two things
that we are covering here.

1093
00:55:02,660 --> 00:55:05,000
So one is the Agentic
RAG, and one is Search-o1.

1094
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:07,680
So I'll cover both here.

1095
00:55:07,680 --> 00:55:10,040
Let's first understand
Agentic RAG because then,

1096
00:55:10,040 --> 00:55:13,280
we build on top of that
in the Search-o1 process.

1097
00:55:13,280 --> 00:55:16,120
In Agentic RAG, what you're
doing is you're basically--

1098
00:55:16,120 --> 00:55:21,380
every time you are hitting
entities that you care about,

1099
00:55:21,380 --> 00:55:24,040
you're basically going to
insert special tokens where you

1100
00:55:24,040 --> 00:55:25,720
are going to trigger a search.

1101
00:55:25,720 --> 00:55:27,220
And then when you
get that document,

1102
00:55:27,220 --> 00:55:29,300
you're going to
insert those results.

1103
00:55:29,300 --> 00:55:32,600
So model is generating
some output tokens.

1104
00:55:32,600 --> 00:55:35,600
When it's seeing certain amount
of uncertainty in its output

1105
00:55:35,600 --> 00:55:37,520
tokens, it's generating
these search queries

1106
00:55:37,520 --> 00:55:39,180
between the special tokens.

1107
00:55:39,180 --> 00:55:41,740
And based on that, it goes
and does the tool call.

1108
00:55:41,740 --> 00:55:44,355
And the retrieval
tool will essentially

1109
00:55:44,355 --> 00:55:46,480
return those documents,
which will then be inserted

1110
00:55:46,480 --> 00:55:47,840
into the reasoning chain.

1111
00:55:47,840 --> 00:55:50,180
So this is happening in
large reasoning models

1112
00:55:50,180 --> 00:55:52,460
as part of the whole
reasoning process of like,

1113
00:55:52,460 --> 00:55:56,380
it's also calling tools as
part of the reasoning process.

1114
00:55:56,380 --> 00:56:01,180
So that's one way to solve it.

1115
00:56:01,180 --> 00:56:03,620
But the documents
can be lengthy,

1116
00:56:03,620 --> 00:56:05,000
and they can contain noise.

1117
00:56:05,000 --> 00:56:07,140
And oftentimes,
depending on how good

1118
00:56:07,140 --> 00:56:10,140
the long-context understanding
and reasoning of the model is,

1119
00:56:10,140 --> 00:56:13,200
they might-- actually, if
you put 10 or 20 documents,

1120
00:56:13,200 --> 00:56:16,100
it might actually not be able
to do a good job at reasoning

1121
00:56:16,100 --> 00:56:16,980
over them.

1122
00:56:16,980 --> 00:56:20,220
So the reason in documents
module of Search-o1

1123
00:56:20,220 --> 00:56:23,580
will allow you to do analysis
of each of the documents of how

1124
00:56:23,580 --> 00:56:26,020
relevant the content
is, and then it

1125
00:56:26,020 --> 00:56:29,780
considers the current query,
what it was reasoning previously

1126
00:56:29,780 --> 00:56:34,980
plus this chunk of information
that it extracted, and appends

1127
00:56:34,980 --> 00:56:37,820
that to the prompt to
allow the model to continue

1128
00:56:37,820 --> 00:56:39,740
reasoning based on that.

1129
00:56:39,740 --> 00:56:42,220
So it's essentially
a multi-step process

1130
00:56:42,220 --> 00:56:45,295
within that retrieval
tool function itself

1131
00:56:45,295 --> 00:56:47,670
because it's basically getting
that document information,

1132
00:56:47,670 --> 00:56:49,490
but then also doing
an extraction process

1133
00:56:49,490 --> 00:56:50,810
on top of that.

1134
00:56:50,810 --> 00:56:52,730
It's as almost as humans.

1135
00:56:52,730 --> 00:56:54,690
You don't want to
just go and curate

1136
00:56:54,690 --> 00:56:56,030
all the possible references.

1137
00:56:56,030 --> 00:56:58,250
You also want to take
notes on those references

1138
00:56:58,250 --> 00:57:02,530
so that you have some way of
doing a better job understanding

1139
00:57:02,530 --> 00:57:04,650
and synthesizing
that information.

1140
00:57:04,650 --> 00:57:08,330
And this allows the model to
do a better job at reasoning

1141
00:57:08,330 --> 00:57:10,930
when we are adding
external knowledge.

1142
00:57:10,930 --> 00:57:12,670
And especially for
complex queries,

1143
00:57:12,670 --> 00:57:14,610
this becomes quite important.

1144
00:57:14,610 --> 00:57:15,230
Yes.

1145
00:57:15,230 --> 00:57:18,110
How is it pausing
previous reasoning?

1146
00:57:18,110 --> 00:57:20,290
And if there's any
corrections needed

1147
00:57:20,290 --> 00:57:23,930
for the previous reasoning,
how does it handle corrections?

1148
00:57:23,930 --> 00:57:26,730
So there is an assumption
that there is a state

1149
00:57:26,730 --> 00:57:30,170
or there is a memory
buffer that's keeping that.

1150
00:57:30,170 --> 00:57:33,470
It's not obvious in the
way this is structured,

1151
00:57:33,470 --> 00:57:37,450
but there is basically
some form of notion of this

1152
00:57:37,450 --> 00:57:39,190
is the search query
I'm trying to answer

1153
00:57:39,190 --> 00:57:43,190
and some buffer of where is the
previous reasoning and so on.

1154
00:57:43,190 --> 00:57:44,390
All of it in the slide.

1155
00:57:44,390 --> 00:57:46,560
Yeah.

1156
00:57:46,560 --> 00:57:47,060
Yes.

1157
00:57:47,060 --> 00:57:53,470
[INAUDIBLE] to prepare some new
form in the Agentic RAG module.

1158
00:57:53,470 --> 00:57:57,190
On top of that, we just ask
